SQL Server Upgrade Guide

Migration from SQL Server 2016 to SQL Server 2019 / 2022

Dedicated Instances with Always On Availability Groups

⸻

Overview

This article describes the recommended approach for upgrading Dedicated SQL Server instances from SQL Server 2016 to a newer supported version (SQL Server 2019 or preferably SQL Server 2022) in environments using Always On Availability Groups with a listener.

The migration approach is based on a side-by-side upgrade strategy, which:
	•	Minimizes downtime during final cutover
	•	Preserves the existing listener connectivity
	•	Maintains infrastructure consistency
	•	Reduces operational and upgrade risk

This guide applies specifically to the Dedicated Instance service model, where customers retain administrative control over SQL Server instance-level configuration.

⸻

Migration Approach

Instead of performing an in-place upgrade, the recommended approach is to:
	1.	Provision new SQL Server instances with the target version.
	2.	Align infrastructure configuration with the existing environment.
	3.	Add the new instances to the existing Availability Group.
	4.	Synchronize databases before cutover.
	5.	Perform a controlled failover to the upgraded environment.

This approach ensures the existing listener remains unchanged, allowing applications to reconnect without connection string modifications.

⸻

Prerequisites (Customer Actions)

Request New SQL Server Instances

Customers should request:
	•	Two Dedicated SQL Server instances
	•	Target version: SQL Server 2019 or preferably SQL Server 2022

Recommended configuration:
	•	Keep the same instance name:
	•	Default instance → MSSQLSERVER
	•	Named instance → reuse the same instance name

This simplifies migration and reduces post-upgrade adjustments.

⸻

Submit Migration Request (ServiceNow)

After provisioning the new instances, submit a ServiceNow request including:

Current environment
	•	Existing SQL Server 2016 instance names
	•	Availability Group name
	•	Listener DNS name used by applications

Target environment
	•	Newly provisioned instances
	•	Preferred listener/CNAME details if changes are planned

Business context
	•	Project or business unit ownership
	•	Preferred maintenance window for failover

