Infrastructure Preparation

Once the migration request is received, the Database Platform Support Team will:

Infrastructure Alignment
	•	Match CPU and memory allocation
	•	Replicate storage layout
	•	Validate performance characteristics

Availability Group Preparation
	•	Join new instances to the Availability Group
	•	Configure database synchronization
	•	Monitor replication health and readiness

Databases are typically synchronized before the planned cutover.

⸻

Final Cutover Process

The final migration phase consists of:
	1.	Planned application downtime.
	2.	Suspension of all write activity to databases.
	3.	Controlled failover to upgraded SQL Server instances.
	4.	Validation of application functionality.

Because the listener remains unchanged:
	•	Applications usually reconnect automatically.
	•	Downtime is limited to the maintenance window.

⸻

Failback and Rollback Considerations

No Standard Failback

After failover to SQL Server 2019/2022:
	•	Failback to SQL Server 2016 is not supported.
	•	Database internal version changes prevent backward synchronization.
	•	This should be considered a one-way upgrade.

⸻

Emergency Rollback Scenario (Limited Conditions)

A rollback may be technically possible only if:
	•	Failover has completed, but
	•	No production write activity has resumed.

In this scenario:
	•	SQL Server 2016 databases remain in restoring state.
	•	They can be removed from the Availability Group.
	•	Database recovery can be performed manually.
	•	The original Availability Group can be rebuilt if required.

This option:
	•	Is operationally complex
	•	Is not guaranteed
	•	Should only be treated as an emergency fallback

⸻

Important Technical Note

Even without application writes, SQL Server may generate internal log records related to:
	•	Statistics updates
	•	Metadata adjustments
	•	Background system processes

If such changes occur, rollback may no longer be feasible.

⸻

Customer Responsibilities Before Cutover

Customers should ensure:
	•	All instance-level objects are migrated:
	•	Logins
	•	SQL Agent jobs
	•	Linked servers
	•	SSIS packages
	•	Credentials and server-level configurations
	•	Applications are tested against the new SQL Server version.
	•	Deprecated features are reviewed.
	•	Operational readiness is confirmed before cutover.

⸻

Summary

This migration approach provides:
	•	Controlled upgrade from SQL Server 2016
	•	Reduced operational risk
	•	Minimal downtime migration capability
	•	Infrastructure consistency
	•	Continued use of existing listener connectivity

Proper preparation on both the platform and customer sides is essential for a successful migration.

